's office  to make sure that those grants are  distributed properly  in addition we're seeing the issue of  eliminating natural gas and that's  reaching the state house now so now  there's  an effort to remove natural gas from  commercial residential buildings  and essentially that would affect  restaurants because that would um  limit their ability to use natural gas  and they would have to essentially  use electric uh stoves so that's  something that really  affects the way the method in which a  lot of our cooks  and our chefs you know what they do on a  daily basis so that's something that is  really really a big issue for us  as well as retrofitting natural gas  current natural gas  equipment to electric which is again  extremely costly  and it's really not a time to increase  other operational costs for our  operators um it's yet another thing that  they have to kind of potentially jump  through and so that's something that  we've been really vocal about up here  at carson city and trying to kind of you  know make sure that the  representation's there for restaurants  you mentioned a few moments ago that you  know the 70 percent of the restaurants  that are still around no one's really  happy  is there you say and also there's a  light at the end of the tunnel  what does the association and its  members  envision for sort of the next  six months in nevada restaurants in  terms of  capacity funding survival  safety etc is if there's a crystal ball  here  what are what are the hopes of the  association members of how this is going  to all play out  sure so if we're looking at the covid uh  plan  we're looking at march 15th which is  this coming monday  as an important day for our restaurant  operators because they'll be able to  operate at 50  capacity indoor which they haven't had  for quite  quite some time i should say um recently  they've been  uh they've been limited to 35  and then before that it was 25 um and  then it was just  you know um take out and cu